{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CggHvzzcsEbcyB4Xi1SjN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fixed. `split_cents(100, 3)` now returns `[34, 33, 33]`, so all 100 grosze are kept.\n\n**The cause:** `split_cents` used floor division for every share and never distributed the remainder. `100 // 3` is 33, and the leftover 1 grosz was dropped.\n\n**The change** (`money.py`): it now uses `divmod` and gives one extra grosz to the first `remainder` people:\n\n```python\nshare, remainder = divmod(total, people)\nreturn [share + 1 if i < remainder else share for i in range(people)]\n```\n\n**Test** (`test_money.py`): I added `test_uneven_split_keeps_every_grosz`, which asserts `[34, 33, 33]` and that the shares sum to 100. I ran it first and saw it fail with `[33, 33, 33]`, then made the fix.\n\n**Verification:**\n- `python3 -m unittest test_money`: all 4 tests pass, including the existing even-split and zero-people tests.\n- I also checked every total from 0 to 499 against 1 to 19 people. Each result has the right length, sums to the total, and no two shares differ by more than 1 grosz.\n- Ruff isn't installed here, so I didn't run a linter.\n\n**Choice I made:** the extra grosze go to the first people in the list, so `split_cents(100, 3)` gives the 34 to the first person. If you'd rather it be deterministic in a different way, such as the last person getting the extra, that's a one-line change."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":969,"cache_read_input_tokens":21708,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":969},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"69abcace-86f1-4378-80db-65e98ee29d81","uuid":"a0af3633-7c38-412f-a